- Best for long, structured, section-heavy business documents
- Source: SEC EDGAR public filings

2. SEC 10-Q quarterly reports
- Good for shorter filings and repeated quarterly structure
- Source: SEC EDGAR public filings

3. SEC 8-K current reports
- Good for event-driven filings and shorter documents
- Source: SEC EDGAR public filings

4. GAO reports
- Strong for government-style structured PDFs
- Good for summaries, factoids, and QA extraction
- Source: GAO publications

5. Congressional hearing transcripts
- Good for long narrative text and Q&A style content
- Source: GovInfo / Congress repositories

6. CFR / government regulation pages
- Good for highly structured section-based documents
- Source: GovInfo / eCFR

7. arXiv research papers
- Good for academic PDFs with sections, equations, references
- Source: arXiv

8. Technical documentation pages
- Good examples: Python docs, PostgreSQL docs, Kubernetes docs
- Best for HTML-to-Markdown conversion and semantic chunking
- Source: official documentation websites

9. Public whitepapers
- Good for mixed prose, charts, and section-based formatting
- Source: company or government public sites

10. Policy documents
- Good for testing long-form summarization and contextual chunking
- Source: government or public policy repositories

